                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  1

Date of Hearing:   March 10, 1999

                ASSEMBLY COMMITTEE ON EDUCATION 
                      Kerry Mazzoni, Chair
         SB 2 X1 (O'Connell) - As Amended: March 8, 1999

 <u> SENATE VOTE </u> :   34-3
 <u> 
SUBJECT </u> : High School Exit Examination

 <u> SUMMARY </u> : Requires the Superintendent of Public Instruction (SPI)  
to begin to develop a high school exit examination covering  
reading, writing and mathematics and requires that the State  
Board of Education adopt an exit exam by October 1, 2000.    
Specifically, <u> this bill </u> : 

1)Requires the SPI, with the approval of the State Board of  
  Education (SBE), to develop a high school exit exam in  
  language arts and mathematics in accordance with the State  
  Board adopted academically rigorous content standards.

2)Requires the SBE to adopt a high school exit examination that  
  is aligned to state content standards by October 1, 2000.

3)Requires that all students in the graduating class of  
  2003-2004, and all later years, pass the exit examination in  
  order to receive a diploma of high school graduation.

4)Requires the SPI to appoint a High School Exit Examination  
  Standards Panel to assist with the development of the test.   
  The panel , among others, must be comprised of teachers,  
  administrators, school board members, and the general public.   
  Members will serve for two years without pay and shall be  
  representative of the state's ethnic, geographical, and  
  cultural diversity and must have gender balance.

5)Requires that the exit examination be field tested prior to  
  implementation to ensure that the examination is free from  
  bias and that its results are valid and reliable.

6)Requires that the exit examination be reviewed by the existing  
  Statewide Pupil Assessment Review (SPAR) Panel to ensure that  
  no questions elicit information concerning personal beliefs or  
  practices.









                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  2

7)Requires that the high school exit exam a) not be administered  
  to a pupil who did not receive adequate notice (as defined)  
  regarding the test; b) not intentionally discriminate against  
  a protected group or class; and c) have instructional and  
  curricular validity (these are similar to standards set forth  
  in Debra P. v. Turlington described below).  If this is not  
  done, the test shall not be required as a condition of  
  graduation.

8)Provides for accommodation of special education students by  
  requiring the development of differential standards and  
  assessment procedures.  This is similar to existing  
  accommodations provided for special needs pupils who are being  
  assessed to local proficiency standards.

9)Requires that each pupil takes the exit exam in grade ten  
  starting in 2002 and be allowed to take the exam at each  
  subsequent administration until each part of the exam has been  
  passed.  Pupils are also allowed, but not required, to take  
  the test in grade nine starting in 2001 . 

10)Allows districts to defer testing of English Language  
  Learners (ELL) until the pupil has been in California schools  
  for two years, during which time the ELL pupil should have  
  received at least six months of instruction in reading,  
  writing and comprehension in the English language.  Deferring  
  test administration does not exempt the ELL pupil from taking  
  the exit test, and the pupil must still pass the test in  
  English in order to graduate.

11) Requires that supplemental instruction be provided to any  
  pupil who "does not demonstrate sufficient progress toward  
  passing the exit examination."   Specifies that sufficient  
  progress shall be determined on the basis of either the  
  results of STAR and the minimum levels of proficiency  
  specified by the SBE or the pupil's grades and other  
  indicators of academic achievement designated by the school  
  district.

12)Effective January 1, 2000 repeals existing requirements for  
  establishment of local proficiency standards and assessment of  
  pupils to those standards. (Repeals Article 2.5 of Chapter 2  
  of Part 28 of the Education Code, commencing with Section  
  51215.)









                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  3

13)Appropriates two million dollars ($2,000,000) of federal  
  Goals 2000 funding to the SPI to develop the high school exit  
  examination.

14)Requires that by January 15, 2000, the SPI shall contract for  
  a multiyear independent evaluation of the high school exit  
  examination.  The evaluation shall be based upon information  
  gathered in field testing and annual administrations of the  
  examination and shall include specified information.

15)Provides that after adoption and initial administration of  
  the high school exit examination the SBE, in consultation with  
  the SPI, shall study the appropriateness of other criteria by  
  which high school pupils who are regarded as highly proficient  
  but unable to pass the high school exit examination may  
  demonstrate their competency and receive a high school  
  diploma.  These criteria may include, but are not limited to,  
  an exemplary academic record as evidenced by transcripts and  
  alternative tests of equal rigor in the academic areas covered  
  by the high school exit examination. If the SBE determines  
  that other criteria are appropriate and do not undermine the  
  intent of this chapter that all high school graduates  
  demonstrate satisfactory academic proficiency, the board shall  
  forward its recommendations to the Legislature for enactment. 

16)Authorizes schools to use regularly available resources and  
  any available supplemental remedial resources to prepare  
  pupils to succeed on the exit exam.

 <u> EXISTING LAW </u> 

 <u> STAR (Standardized Testing and Reporting)  (SB 376 /1997) </u> .  All  
students in grades 2 through 11 are tested in the spring of each  
year on a standardized, commercially published  
("off-the-shelf"), test of basic skills that is designated by  
the State Board of Education.  The Board has currently  
designated the Stanford Achievement Test, form 9, (SAT9 or  
Stanford 9) test.  This test provides individual student scores  
that are cross-referenced to the publisher's national test  
taking population ("national norm"). Current law a) requires  
that parents and guardians of pupils receive their students'  
results from the designated test; b) requires the test publisher  
to post the grade, school, school district and state scores from  
the statewide test (aggregated and disaggregated by gender, LEP  
status, and economically disadvantaged status, on the Internet  








                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  4

every year by June 30); and c) requires that the publisher  
provide such scores to teachers, administrators, school and  
county boards and SDE.  

 <u> Local Proficiency Tests  (AB 65 /1977) </u> .  All school districts  
maintaining any of the grades 6 through 12 are required to  
establish basic skills proficiency standards and administer  
proficiency assessments (usually tests) that all students must  
pass in order to graduate.  Tests and standards are locally  
developed and aligned to local curriculum, provided that they  
address, at a minimum, "reading comprehension, writing and  
computational skills, in the English language".  Different  
standards and testing procedures are authorized for special  
education pupils and other pupils with a diagnosed learning  
disability.  Assessment of pupil proficiency is required at  
least once each during grades 4 through 6, and 7 through 9, and  
twice during grades 10 and 11.  Pupils of limited English  
proficiency (LEP) are not tested until they have received  
at least 24 months of instruction, including six months  
instruction in English.

 <u> English language learners </u> . Requires school districts, charter  
schools and county offices of education to annually assess  
English learners in their primary language, for the first year  
that these children enter public school in California, if such a  
test is available.  Current law also allows these students to be  
tested after the first year and provides schools with funding to  
reimburse them for their costs of doing so.  Legislation  
approved last year requires the State Board of Education to  
designate a single primary language test for use in grades 2-11,  
if such a test is available.  

 <u> Current Graduation Requirements </u> .  Current law requires  
California students to complete state and locally specified  
courses in order to graduate from high school.  Although  
attainment of local proficiency standards (described above) is  
required, there is currently no single statewide test required  
for high school graduation.

 <u> Property Rights and Test Design </u> .   In a lawsuit (Debra P. v.  
Turlington) that challenged the state of Florida over their exit  
exam, a federal Court of Appeals determined that students have a  
legal property interest in receiving a diploma (thus the state  
must have a legally defensible position if it denies a diploma  
to an otherwise qualified person).  The court laid out three  








                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  5

principles that should be considered for high school exit exams.

1)The test must not intentionally discriminate against a  
  protected group or class.

2)A graduation test must be a fair measure of what students have  
  been taught, that is, it must have "curricular validity".

3)Students must have adequate advance notice of test  
  requirements.

 <u> FISCAL EFFECT </u> : $2 million appropriation of Federal Goals 2000  
funds.  Unknown state administration costs and independent  
evaluation costs.

 <u> COMMENTS </u>:   

 <u> Governor's Education Reform Proposal. </u>  This bill is one of four  
bills sponsored by the Governor as part of his call for an  
extraordinary session of the legislature dedicated to education  
reform issues.  The other three bills address accountability (SB  
1X Alpert), peer assistance and review for teachers (AB 1X  
Villaraigosa), and reading reform (AB 2X Mazzoni).

 <u> Significant amendments made in bill to date </u> .  Many significant  
amendments have been made to the bill to accommodate various  
concerns.  They include:

 Various provisions to insure that the exam meets federal  
  court requirements;
 The SBE shall study alternative tests for students, who are  
  good students but who can't pass the exit exam, that they  
  might take/pass in order to receive a high school diploma;
 Supplementary instruction is required for students who need  
  help in passing the exam to be funded by redirecting  
  existing regular and remedial funding;
 Creation of a broadly based advisory committee to the SBE  
  conceding the content of the exit exam;
 Requirement of a comprehensive evaluation of the effects of  
  the exit exam;
 Aaccommodations for special education students and English  
  language learners.

 <u> Does the bill comply with federal special education  
requirements </u> ?  Concern has been expressed that section 60850 (g)  








                                                         <u> SB 2 X1
                                                         </u> Page  6

(page 7, line 32) may be out of compliance with the federal IDEA  
requirements.  Specifically, the bill provides that "An  
individual with exceptional needs to whom the examination cannot  
be administered, as documented in his or her individualized  
education program, shall be given an alternate examination that  
is adopted by the governing board of the school district and  
meets criteria established by the State Board of Education."   
Advocates argue that the alternative examination must be  
developed by the pupil's IEP team with parent participation and  
not at the local board level.

 <u> Clarification needed. </u>  The bill provides that if the SBE  
determines that other criteria are appropriate and do not  
undermine the intent of this chapter that all high school  
graduates demonstrate satisfactory academic proficiency, the  
board shall forward its recommendations to the Legislature <u> for  
enactment </u> .  Since one legislature can not bind another, it might  
be more appropriate to say <u> for consideration </u> .

 <u> Related legislation </u> .  AB 125 and AB 3X (both by Campbell),  
require an exit exam to be administered to the class of 2001-02.  
 At the request of the author, these bills are not being heard  
at this time.

 <u> REGISTERED SUPPORT / OPPOSITION </u> :  (as of 3/5/99)

 <u> Support </u> 

None on file.
